    "origin_url": "https://electrek.co/2026/11/06/bmw-ix3-crushes-epa-range-driving-400-miles-real-world-test/",
    "collected_at": "2026-11-06T20:12:48.138691+00:00",
    "origin_id": "https://electrek.co/2026/11/06/bmw-ix3-crushes-epa-range-driving-400-miles-real-world-test/",
    "canonical_url": "https://electrek.co/2026/11/06/bmw-ix3-crushes-epa-range-driving-400-miles-real-world-test/",
    "origin_publisher": "Electrek",
    "published_at": "2026-11-06T17:11:26+00:00",
    "published_at_raw": "2026-11-06T17:11:26+00:00",
    "publication_precision": "TIMESTAMP",
    "acquired_at": "2026-11-06T20:22:11.873116+00:00",
    "available_at": "2026-11-06T20:22:11.873116+00:00",
    "body": "BMW’s new iX3 outperformed the Volvo EX60 and Mercedes-Benz GLC EV, driving well over 400 miles during a real-world range test.\nBMW iX3 beats EPA range rating in real-world test\nIn a recent nationally representative survey , BMW found that Americans want more EV range than they actually need.\nThe study found that 73% consider 75 miles of range, or less, enough to cover their daily driving. Meanwhile, 42% said they would require at least 300 miles of range to consider an EV.\nAnother 53% said they would be more likely to consider an EV if it could add about 150 miles of range in 10 minutes.\nAdvertisement - scroll for more content\nBMW’s new iX3 checks all the boxes. Based on BMW’s sixth-generation (Gen6) eDrive 800V system, the 2027 iX3 50 xDrive delivers an EPA-estimated range of up to 434 miles (with 20″ summer tires).\nWith the 22″ summer tires, the iX3 50 xDrive has an EPA-estimated range of up to 392 miles. Still, plenty for at least a week of commuting.\nBMW iX3 50 xDrive (Source: BMW)\nIt can also add 170-185 miles of range in 10 minutes with up to 400 kW DC fast charging. In everyday, real-world driving, it may drive even further than its official EPA numbers suggest.\nDuring Edmunds ‘ EV Range test , the 2027 BMW iX3 50 xDrive (with 22″ summer tires) drove 436 miles on a single charge, topping its EPA estimate of 392 miles by 44 miles.\nThe electric SUV was also more efficient than advertised. The iX3 achieved an energy consumption of 28.3 kWh per 100 miles, beating its EPA estimate of 32 kWh per 100 miles by roughly 11.6%.\nBMW iX3 50 xDrive (Source: BMW)\nBMW’s new iX3 is now the longest-range luxury electric SUV that Edmunds has tested, outperforming rivals including the 2027 Volvo EX60 (352 miles) and Mercedes-Benz GLC Electric (392 miles).\nThe Edmunds real-world range test is designed to provide a more accurate estimate of an EV’s range in everyday driving conditions.\nBMW iX3 50 xDrive interior (Source: BMW)\nIt consists of 60% city and 40% highway driving with an average speed of 40 mph. The vehicle stays within 5 mph of the posted speed limit, and the climate control is set to auto at 72 degrees.\nThe 2027 BMW iX3 50 xDrive is available to order in Sport, M Sport, and M Sport Professional trims, priced from $61,500, $64,000, and $65,500, respectively.\n2027 BMW iX3 50 xDrive trim | Starting Price *\nSport | $61,500\nM Sport | $64,000\nM Sport Professional | $65,500\n2027 BMW iX3 50 xDrive price by trim (*Does not include a $1,350 destination and handling fee)\nTop comment by Dan B\nLiked by 2 people\nIf you go to the Lucid article on here, you will see what I said there about efficiency. So a few thoughts I have here.\nFirst, they got this from a vehicle with a 108.7 KWH battery pack. My 2026 Equinox EV has a listed 85 KWH battery pack and if I charge to 100% and drive like they do in these tests, I would see about 340 miles of range. I know, I've done it. But because I don't need that much range, I now only charge to 85% and still end up with a usable 305 miles of range. My point isn't that the Equinox is better. My point is that they (BMW) are doing this with a much larger battery. Once again, this comes down to sticking a larger battery in and not about concentrating on efficiency.\nSecond, it's so strange that people are requiring so much range. The well known average driver commutes less than 40 miles per day (I think the actual number is 37 miles??). But if we bump that up to 40 miles per day average and figure all 7 days per week, that's 280 miles range needed. So still 20 miles shy of that magic 300 miles of range. Just imagine what would happen if they concentrated more on efficiency (by design, less weight and size) and instead turned that effort towards smaller battery packs with less cost and weight.\nView all comments\nPowered by a 108.7 kWh battery, the new iX3 with 20″ Summer Tires delivers up to 434 miles of range on a single charge.\n2027 BMW iX3 Wheel & Tire Package | MPGe Combined | Range (mi)\n20″ Summer Tires (No-cost option) | 118 | 434\n20″ All-Season Tires (Standard) | 102 | 383\n21″ Summer Tires | 105 | 398\n21″ All-Season Tires | 105 | 399\n22″ Summer Tires | 104 | 392\n2027 BMW iX3 driving range by tire size\nWith a charging rate of up to 400 kW, the new BMW iX3 can recharge from 10% to 80% in about 21 minutes, or add about 185 miles in 10 minutes.\nInside, the electric SUV features the Neue Klasse interior design. The setup includes BMW’s new Panoramic Vision, a full-length windshield display, and a 17.9″ central infotainment screen running the latest Operating System X. Buyers can also upgrade to the optional BMW 3D Head-Up Display.\nWant to take a closer look? We can help you get started. You can use our link here to find available 2027 BMW iX3 50 xDrive models near you , along with any local offers.\nFTC: We use income earning auto affiliate links. More.\nStay up to date with the latest content by subscribing to Electrek on Google News . You’re reading Electrek— experts who break news about Tesla , electric vehicles, and green energy , day after day. Be sure to check out our homepage for all the latest news, and follow Electrek on X , Facebook , and LinkedIn to stay in the loop. Don’t know where to start? Check out our YouTube channel for the latest reviews.",
